nts of the sides, the feet of the altitudes, and the midpoints of the segments from each vertex to the orthocenter. The center of the nine-point circle, N, is the midpoint of OH.\n\nAlso, the nine-point circle has a radius half of the circumradius. Therefore, the nine-point circle is a scaled-down version of the circumcircle by a factor of 1/2, centered at N.\n\nMoreover, the centroid G, the circumcenter O, and the nine-point center N are colinear, with NG = 1/3 NO. Wait, if N is the midpoint of OH, then ON = NH. If G divides OH such that OG : GH = 1:2, then the midpoint N of OH would be located at O + 1/2 OH = O + 1/2 (3 OG) = O + (3/2) OG. But since OG = 1/3 OH, this might not directly help.\n\nAlternatively, since the nine-point circle center N is the midpoint of OH, and since the nine-point circle is a homothety of the circumcircle with factor 1/2 centered at N, perhaps there's a relation involving G.\n\nAlternatively, going back to vectors. Let me consider position vectors relative to an arbitrary origin. Let\u2019s denote the position vectors of A, B, C as a, b, c. Then, centroid G is (a + b + c)/3.\n\nThe circumcenter O can be found as the solution to the perpendicular bisector equations, but it's complex. The orthocenter H can be expressed as a + b + c - 2O. Wait, where does this formula come from?\n\nWait, let me recall that in a triangle, the vector from the circumcenter to the orthocenter is equal to 3 times the vector from the centroid to the circumcenter. So, OH = 3 OG. Which, in vectors, would be h - o = 3(g - o), assuming all vectors are position vectors relative to the same origin.\n\nSolving for h: h = o + 3g - 3o = 3g - 2o. Therefore, h = 3g - 2o. Which is the same as H = 3G - 2O.\n\nTherefore, this shows that the vector from O to H is 3 times the vector from O to G, hence they are colinear, and OG : GH = 1:2.\n\nBut how do we derive that OH = 3 OG?\n\nLet me think about the properties of the Euler line again. One method to establish this is to use