n vector of H relative to O is a + b + c.\n\nBut the centroid G has position vector (a + b + c)/3. Therefore, OH = 3 OG. Thus, vector OH = 3 vector OG. This implies that O, G, H are colinear, with G lying between O and H, and OG : GH = 1:2. Hence, OG = 1/2 GH.\n\nTherefore, this proves the required result.\n\nThe key step here is recognizing that in vector terms, with O as the origin, the orthocenter H is simply the sum of the position vectors of the vertices, and the centroid G is their average. Therefore, H = 3G, which gives the collinearity and ratio.\n\nThis seems to be the most straightforward vector proof. Therefore, the Euler line's properties are established by this vector relationship.\n\nSo, summarizing the proof:\n\n1. Place the circumcenter O at the origin of the coordinate system.\n2. The position vectors of the vertices A, B, C are a, b, c, each with magnitude equal to the circumradius R.\n3. The centroid G has position vector (a + b + c)/3.\n4. The orthocenter H has position vector a + b + c.\n5. Therefore, H = 3G, which shows that O (origin), G, and H are colinear.\n6. The distance from O to G is |G| = |a + b + c|/3.\n7. The distance from G to H is |H - G| = |3G - G| = |2G| = 2|G|.\n8. Therefore, OG = |G| and GH = 2|G|, so OG = (1/2) GH.\n\nHence, the collinearity and the ratio are proven.\n\nAnother way to express this without placing O at the origin is:\n\nGiven any triangle, the vector from the circumcenter O to the orthocenter H is equal to three times the vector from O to the centroid G. Mathematically, this is:\n\nOH = 3 OG\n\nWhich can be rearranged as:\n\nH - O = 3(G - O)\n\nH = 3G - 2O\n\nThis equation shows that H lies on the line through O and G, specifically such that G divides the segment OH in the ratio OG : GH = 1:2. Therefore, OG = 1/2 GH.\n\nThis completes the proof.", "deepseek_solution": "The circumcenter \\( O \\), centroid \\( G \\), and orthocenter \\( H \\) of any triangle \\( ABC \\) are collinear on the Euler line, and \\( OG = \\frac{1}{2}GH \\). Here's the step-by-step explanation:\n\n1. **Vector Setup**:\n   - Place the circumcenter \\( O \\) at the origin. The position vectors of vertices \\( A \\), \\( B \\), and \\( C \\) are \\( \\vec{a} \\), \\( \\vec{b} \\), and \\( \\vec{c} \\), respectively.\n   - The centroid \\( G \\) has position vector \\( \\vec{G} = \\frac{\\vec{a} + \\vec{b} + \\vec{c}}{3} \\).\n   - The orthocenter \\( H \\) in this coordinate system is given by \\( \\vec{H} = \\vec{a} + \\vec{b} + \\vec{c} \\).\n\n2. **Relationship Between \\( H \\) and \\( G \\)**:\n   - From the expressions above: \\( \\vec{H} = 3\\vec{G} \\).\n   - This implies \\( H \\) is located at three times the vector from the origin \\( O \\) to \\( G \\).\n\n3. **Collinearity**:\n   - Since \\( \\vec{H} = 3\\vec{G} \\), points \\( O \\), \\( G \\), and \\( H \\) lie on a straight line (the Euler line), with \\( G \\) between \\( O \\) and \\( H \\).\n\n4. **Distance Ratio**:\n   - The distance \\( OG \\) is the magnitude \\( |\\vec{G}| \\).\n   - The distance \\( GH \\) is \\( |\\vec{H} - \\vec{G}| = |3\\vec{G} - \\vec{G}| = 2|\\vec{G}| \\).\n   - Thus, \\( OG = \\frac{1}{2}GH \\).\n\n**Conclusion**: \\( O \\), \\( G \\), and \\( H \\) are collinear, and the centroid \\( G \\) divides the segment \\( OH \\) such that \\( OG = \\frac{1}{2}GH \\). This relationship holds for any triangle, proving the properties of the Euler line.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{OG = \\frac{1}{2}GH}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. Consid\u00e9rons un triangle $ABC$ quelconque, avec $A_{1}$, $B_{1}$ et $C_{1}$ les pieds des m\u00e9diatrices respectivement partant de $A$, $B$ et $C$.\n\n2. S'appliquant l'homoth\u00e9tie $h$ de centre $G$ (le centre de gravit\u00e9 du triangle) et de rapport $-\\frac{1}{2}$, nous appelons $O_{-1}$ l'image r\u00e9ciproque de $O$ par cette homoth\u00e9tie, c'est-\u00e0-dire $O_{-1}$ tel que $h^{-1}(O) = O_{-1}$.\n\n3. La propri\u00e9t\u00e9 d'une homoth\u00e9tie avec un rapport $k$ stipule que si $h(P) = P'$, alors pour toute droite passant par $G$, contenant $P$ et $P'$, nous avons \\(GP' \\| GP\\) avec $(G P') = k \\cdot (G P)$.\n\n4. Puisque $h$ applique $A$ sur $A_{1}$, en d'autres termes $A_{1} = h(A)$, nous savons que:\n   \\[\n   h(A) h(O_{-1}) \\| A O_{-1}\n   \\]\n   soit,\n   \\[\n   A_{1} O \\| A O_{-1}\n   \\]\n\n5. Notez que $G$ se trouve au tiers de la longueur du segment $A_{1}A$ et deux fois plus proche de $A$ (car l'homoth\u00e9tie $h$ implique un rapport $-\\frac{1}{2}$). Donc $A_{1}O \\| AO_{-1}$ implique:\n   \\[\n   A_{1} O \\perp BC\n   \\]\n   \n6. Puisque $A_{1}O$ est la m\u00e9diatrice du segment $BC$, il est perpendiculaire \u00e0 $BC$, ce qui entra\u00eene que $AO_{-1}$ est aussi perpendiculaire \u00e0 $BC$. \n\n7. De plus, une analyse similaire pour$BO$ et $CO$ donne que:\n   \\[\n   BO_{-1} \\perp AC \\quad \\text{et} \\quad CO_{-1} \\perp AB\n   \\]\n\n8. Ainsi, $O_{-1}$ appartient simultan\u00e9ment \u00e0 deux hauteurs quelconques du triangle, ce qui le place sur l'orthocentre $H$. Par cons\u00e9quent, nous avons:\n   \\[\n   O_{-1} = H\n   \\]\n\n9. Nous concluons donc que les points $O$, $G$ et $H$ sont colin\u00e9aires avec $OG = \\frac{1}{2} GH$.\n\n$$\\boxed{\\text{Les points $O$, $G$ et $H$ sont colin\u00e9aires et $OG = \\frac{1}{2}GH$.}}$$", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Prove that: \na) A rotation around point $O$ by an angle $\\alpha$ is equivalent to the sequential application of two reflections, where the axes of reflection pass through point $O$ and the angle between these axes is $\\alpha / 2$. A translation is equivalent to two reflections with parallel axes.\nb) Two sequential rotations, one around point $O_{1}$ by an angle $\\alpha$ and another around point $O_{2}$ by an angle $\\beta$ ( $0 \\leqslant \\alpha<2 \\pi, 0 \\leqslant \\beta<2 \\pi$, both rotations are in the same direction), are equivalent to a single rotation by an angle $