\nSo, I = ( (2k(m + c)) / (2k + b + c ), (2k n ) / (2k + b + c ) )\n\nNow, the midpoints:\n\nM_a = midpoint BC: (k, 0 )\n\nM_b = midpoint AC: ( (m + 2k)/2, n / 2 )\n\nM_c = midpoint AB: ( m / 2, n / 2 )\n\nWe need to set the distance from I to M_b equal to the distance from I to M_c.\n\nCompute distance squared from I to M_b:\n\n[ ( (2k(m + c))/(2k + b + c ) - (m + 2k)/2 )\u00b2 + ( (2k n )/(2k + b + c ) - n / 2 )\u00b2 ]\n\nSimilarly, distance squared from I to M_c:\n\n[ ( (2k(m + c))/(2k + b + c ) - m / 2 )\u00b2 + ( (2k n )/(2k + b + c ) - n / 2 )\u00b2 ]\n\nSetting these equal:\n\n[Term1_b] + [Term2_b] = [Term1_c] + [Term2_c]\n\nCompute Term1_b:\n\n[ (2k(m + c) - (m + 2k)(2k + b + c ) / 2 ) / (2k + b + c ) ]\u00b2\n\nSimilarly, Term1_c:\n\n[ (2k(m + c) - m(2k + b + c ) / 2 ) / (2k + b + c ) ]\u00b2\n\nTerm2_b and Term2_c:\n\nTerm2_b = [ (2k n - n (2k + b + c ) / 2 ) / (2k + b + c ) ]\u00b2\n\nTerm2_c = Term2_b, since the y-coordinate part is the same for both distances:\n\nWait, no. Let's check:\n\nFor M_b, the y-coordinate is n/2, so the difference in y is (2k n / (2k + b + c )) - n/2 = n ( 2k / (2k + b + c ) - 1/2 )\n\nFor M_c, the y-coordinate is also n/2, same as M_b. Wait, no, M_c is midpoint of AB, which is (m/2, n/2 ). Similarly, M_b is midpoint of AC, which is ( (m + 2k)/2, n/2 ). So both M_b and M_c have the same y-coordinate n/2. Therefore, the y-component of the distance from I to both M_b and M_c is the same: | I_y - n/2 |. Therefore, Term2_b = Term2_c. Therefore, when we set the distances equal, the y-terms cancel out, and we only need the x-terms to satisfy the equality.\n\nTherefore, the equation reduces to:\n\n[Term1_b] = [Term1_c]\n\nTherefore:\n\n[ (2k(m + c) - (m + 2k)(2k + b + c ) / 2 ) / (2k + b + c ) ]\u00b2 = [ (2k(m + c) - m(2k + b + c ) / 2 ) / (2k + b + c ) ]\u00b2\n\nSince denominators are the same and positive, we can equate the numerators squared:\n\n[2k(m + c) - (m + 2k)(2k + b + c ) / 2 ]\u00b2 = [2k(m + c) - m(2k + b + c ) / 2 ]\u