, that's right. So f\u2019(y) = sin(2y + z) cos z.\n\nTherefore, f\u2019(y) = sin(2y + z) cos z.\n\nTo find critical points, set f\u2019(y) = 0. So sin(2y + z) cos z = 0.\n\nBut cos z is not zero since z \u2208 [\u03c0/12, \u03c0/6], which is less than \u03c0/2. Therefore, sin(2y + z) = 0.\n\nBut sin\u03b8 = 0 when \u03b8 = k\u03c0. However, 2y + z must be in some range. Let's see:\n\nSince y \u2208 [z, (\u03c0/2 - z)/2], then 2y \u2208 [2z, \u03c0/2 - z], so 2y + z \u2208 [3z, \u03c0/2]. Since z \u2265 \u03c0/12, 3z \u2265 \u03c0/4. And \u03c0/2 is 6\u03c0/12. So 3z \u2208 [\u03c0/4, \u03c0/2]. Therefore, 2y + z \u2208 [\u03c0/4, \u03c0/2], but sin\u03b8 is positive in [0, \u03c0], so sin(2y + z) = 0 only if 2y + z = \u03c0, but 2y + z is at most \u03c0/2, so sin(2y + z) cannot be zero in this interval. Therefore, there are no critical points inside the interval y \u2208 [z, (\u03c0/2 - z)/2].\n\nTherefore, the extrema must occur at the endpoints of the interval for y. That is, either y = z or y = (\u03c0/2 - z)/2.\n\nSo, for each z, the maximum and minimum of f(y, z) occur at y = z or y = (\u03c0/2 - z)/2.\n\nTherefore, we can reduce the problem to optimizing over z by considering these two cases.\n\nCase 1: y = z\n\nThen, since x + y + z = \u03c0/2, x = \u03c0/2 - 2z.\n\nBut we have the constraints x \u2265 y = z and z \u2265 \u03c0/12. So x = \u03c0/2 - 2z \u2265 z \u21d2 \u03c0/2 - 2z \u2265 z \u21d2 \u03c0/2 \u2265 3z \u21d2 z \u2264 \u03c0/6. Which is already satisfied since z \u2264 \u03c0/6 as we saw before.\n\nAlso, z \u2265 \u03c0/12. So in this case, z \u2208 [\u03c0/12, \u03c0/6].\n\nThen, the product becomes cos x sin y cos z = cos(\u03c0/2 - 2z) sin z cos z = sin(2z) sin z cos z.\n\nWait, cos(\u03c0/2 - 2z) = sin(2z). So:\n\nf(z) = sin(2z) sin z cos z\n\n= sin(2z) * sin z cos z\n\nBut sin(2z) = 2 sin z cos z, so:\n\nf(z) = 2 sin z cos z * sin z cos z = 2 sin\u00b2z cos\u00b2z\n\nAlternatively, f(z) = 2 sin\u00b2z cos\u00b2z = (1/2) sin\u00b2(2z)\n\nWa