02B=0:\n\n2B - (2/25)(10 - A - B)=0 \u21922B= (2/25)(10 - A - B)\u2192Multiply by25:\n\n50B=2(10 - A - B)\u219250B=20 -2A -2B\u219250B +2A +2B=20\u21922A +52B=20\u2192A +26B=10\n\nNow we have two equations:\n\n26A + B =10,\n\nA +26B =10.\n\nSubtract the second equation from the first:\n\n25A -25B =0 \u2192A = B.\n\nSubstitute A = B into first equation:\n\n26A + A =10\u219227A=10\u2192A=10/27\u22480.37037.\n\nThus, A = B =10/27.\n\nThen, from A + B \u226410, we have 10/27 +10/27 =20/27\u22480.7407 \u226410, which is true.\n\nNow, check if this minimum is attainable given the constraints on x_P, y_P, x_Q, y_Q.\n\nWe need to check if there exist x_P, y_P, x_Q, y_Q \u22650 with x_P + y_P \u22645, x_Q + y_Q \u22645, such that x_P +x_Q =10/27 and y_P + y_Q=10/27.\n\nYes, since 10/27 \u22480.37037, which is less than 5, we can set x_P = x_Q =5/27\u22480.185 and y_P = y_Q=5/27\u22480.185. Then x_P + y_P =10/27\u22480.37037 \u22645, which is true. So such points exist.\n\nTherefore, the minimal value of f is:\n\nA\u00b2 + B\u00b2 + [(10 -A -B)/5]^2 = (10/27)^2 + (10/27)^2 + [(10 -20/27)/5]^2.\n\nCompute each term:\n\nFirst term: (10/27)^2 =100/729\n\nSecond term same: 100/729\n\nThird term: (10 -20/27)/5 = (270/27 -20/27)/5 = (250/27)/5=50/27. Then squared: (50/27)^2=2500/729\n\nTherefore, total f =100/729 +100/729 +2500/729=2700/729=2700 \u00f7729= 100/27\u22483.7037\n\nSo the minimal distance squared is100/27, so the minimal distance is10/\u221a27=10/(3\u221a3)= (10\u221a3)/9\u22481.9245.\n\nBut wait, this is the same as the distance between the two planes earlier. However, this result assumes that the minimal distance occurs at A=B=10/27, which corresponds to specific points P and Q on ABC. However, we need to verify if these points P and Q are indeed on ABC.\n\nGiven that x_P +x_Q=10/27 and y_P +y_Q=10/27, with x_P, y_P, x_Q, y_Q \u22650, and x_P + y_P \u22645, x_Q + y_Q \u22645.\n\nIf we take x_P =x_Q=5/27 and y_P=y_Q=5/27, then:\n\nFor point P: x_P=5/27, y_P=5/27, so z