h one vertex in between is longer than the side length. So the triangle would have two sides equal (the sides of the pentagon) and the third side longer. Therefore, it's isosceles. Similarly, if you take non-consecutive vertices in a regular pentagon, say with two vertices apart, then you might get different lengths. Wait, no. Let me think.\n\nIn a regular pentagon, all edges are equal, but the diagonals are longer. If you take three vertices A, B, C where AB and BC are edges, then ABC is an isosceles triangle with AB=BC. If you take A, B, D where D is two vertices away from B, then AB is an edge, BD is a diagonal, and AD is another diagonal. Wait, in a regular pentagon, all diagonals are equal. So triangle ABD would have sides AB, BD, AD. AB is a side, BD and AD are diagonals. Since diagonals in a regular pentagon are all equal, BD = AD, so triangle ABD is isosceles with BD = AD. Similarly, any three vertices in a regular pentagon form an isosceles triangle. Wait, is that true?\n\nWait, let's check. In a regular pentagon, the distance between any two non-adjacent vertices (the diagonal) is the same. So if I pick three vertices, the distances between them can be either sides or diagonals. If two of the distances are sides and one is a diagonal, then it's isosceles if two sides are equal. If all three distances are diagonals, then since all diagonals are equal, it's equilateral, hence isosceles. If two are diagonals and one is a side, then it's isosceles only if two diagonals are equal, which they are. Wait, in a regular pentagon, all diagonals are equal. So any triangle formed by three vertices would either have two sides and one diagonal (with the two sides equal) or three diagonals (equilateral). So yes, in a regular pentagon, every three vertices form an isosceles triangle. Interesting.\n\nTherefore, regular polygons might have the property that any three vertices form an isosceles triangle. Let's confirm for n=5 (regular pentagon). Let me take specific points. L