Notice that \\( BO < AB \\) and \\( BO < BC \\).\n   - By the problem conditions, triangles \\( \\triangle ABO \\) and \\( \\triangle BCO \\) are isosceles, thus \\( OB = OA \\) and \\( OB = OC \\).\n   - Therefore, \\( OA = OB = OC \\), indicating that \\( O \\) is the center of the circle circumscribing \\( \\triangle ABC \\).\n\n3. **Proof by Induction on \\( n \\)**:\n   - Base Case: \\( n = 3 \\)\n     - In the case of a triangle (\\( n = 3 \\)), all \\( k \\) points must lie inside the triangle, and thus, by the lemma, they must coincide with the center of the circumscribed circle of this triangle.\n     - Therefore, \\( k \\leq 1 \\).\n\n   - Inductive Step: Suppose for \\( n \\geq 3 \\), the claim holds that \\( k \\leq 1 \\).\n\n     - Now consider a convex \\( n \\)-gon where \\( n \\geq 4 \\). The \\( n \\)-gon must have at least one internal or external angle \\( \\angle A_{i-1} A_i A_{i+1} \\) that is either a right angle or an obtuse angle.\n     - By the lemma, none of the \\( k \\) points can lie within the triangle \\( \\triangle A_{i-1} A_i A_{i+1} \\).\n     - If we cut the \\( n \\)-gon by removing the triangle \\( \\triangle A_{i-1} A_i A_{i+1} \\), we form an \\((n-1)\\)-gon that contains \\( k \\) points.\n     - By the induction hypothesis, for this \\((n-1)\\)-gon, \\( k \\leq 1 \\). Therefore, it follows for our original \\( n \\)-gon that \\( k \\leq 1 \\).\n\n4. **Conclusion**:\n   - Examples verifying \\( k = 0 \\) and \\( k = 1 \\) are straightforward:\n     - For \\( k = 0 \\), there are no additional points inside the \\( n \\)-gon.\n     - For \\( k = 1 \\), place the one point at the center of the circumcircle of an isosceles triangle formed by any three points of the \\( n \\)-gon.\n\nThus, we conclude \\( k \\) can only be \\( 0 \\) or \\( 1 \\).\n\\[ \\boxed{k = 0 \\text{ or } 1} \\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "A set of 20 random digits is given. Determine t