t one even digit, the probability that there is at least one 5 is 1 minus the probability that none of the 20 digits is 5. But the presence of even digits doesn't affect the presence of 5s, since even digits and 5s are in separate categories. \n\nWait, actually, no. If you have at least one even digit, the rest of the digits can be anything except 0. So the presence of even digits doesn't influence the probability of having a 5. So the two events (having at least one even and having at least one 5) are actually independent? \n\nWait, no. If the digits are chosen independently, but once you condition on having at least one even, does that affect the probability of having at least one 5? For example, if you know that in the 20 digits, at least one is even, does that change the probability that there is at least one 5? \n\nIn general, if two events are independent, then conditioning on one does not affect the probability of the other. But here, are they independent?\n\nThe presence of even digits and presence of 5s. Let's see. Let\u2019s model each digit as being in one of three categories: even (2,4,6,8), 5, or other odd (1,3,7,9). Each digit is selected with probability 4/10 for even, 1/10 for 5, and 4/10 for other odd. Wait, but if we're in the case where digits are non-zero, then probabilities are 4/9 for even, 1/9 for 5, and 4/9 for other odd. \n\nSo in the non-zero case, each digit has a 4/9 chance of being even, 1/9 chance of being 5, and 4/9 chance of being other odd. Therefore, the presence of a 5 is independent from the presence of an even digit? Wait, no. Because if you have a 5, that digit is an odd digit, so it doesn't affect the even digits. However, when considering multiple digits, the presence of at least one even and at least one 5 can be dependent. \n\nBut in reality, since each digit is independent, the events \"at least one even\" and \"at least one 5\" are actually independent? Wait, no. For example, if you have a digit that's a 5, that doesn't influence whether another digit is even. So perhaps they are independent. Wait, if events A and B are independent, then P(A \u2229 B) = P(A) * P(B). Let's check with the two-digit example. \n\nIn the two-digit non-zero case, P(at least one even) is 1 - (5/9)^2 = 56/81. P(at least one 5) is 1 - (8/9)^2 = 17/81. If they were independent, then P(A \u2229 B) should be 56/81 * 17/81 \u2248 0.113. But we know the actual P(A \u2229 B) is 8/81 \u2248 0.0988, which is different. Therefore, they are not independent. \n\nTherefore, the user's approach of multiplying P(at least one even) by P(at least one 5 given at least one even) is correct in principle, but their calculation of P(at least one 5 given at least one even) is wrong. \n\nWait, how should we compute P(at least one 5 | at least one even)?\n\nThis is equal to 1 - P(no 5s | at least one even). \n\nBut how do we compute P(no 5s | at least one even)?\n\nThis is equal to [P(no 5s and at least one even)] / P(at least one even)\n\nBut P(no 5s and at least one even) = P(at least one even) - P(at least one even and at least one 5) ?\n\nWait, no. Wait, P(no 5s and at least one even) is equal to P(at least one even) - P(at least one even and at least one 5). But this seems recursive.\n\nAlternatively, since the digits are non-zero, and we have to compute the probability that none of them is 5 and at least one is even.\n\nWhich is equal to [1 - P(all digits are non-5 and non-even)] - P(all digits are non-5 and odd)\n\nWait, maybe not. Let me think. \n\nIf we have no 5s, then all digits are either even or other odds (1,3,7,9). So, the probability of no 5s is (8/9)^20. The probability of no 5s and at least one even is equal to (8/9)^20 - (4/9)^20. Because (8/9)^20 is the probability of all digits being non-5 (so either even or other odds), and (4/9)^20 is the probability that all digits are other odds (no 5s and no evens). Therefore, P(no 5s and at least one even) = (8/9)^20 - (4/9)^20. \n\nTherefore, P(no 5s | at least one even) 