ematically satisfy the conditions. However, placing the sheets with the longer side (8 cm) vertical aligns more naturally with the table\u2019s 80 cm height, minimizing the number of sheets and overlapping.\n\nThus, the length \\( x \\) of the table is **77 cm**.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{77}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Understanding the problem**:\n    - We have a rectangular table of dimensions \\( x \\) cm by 80 cm.\n    - We place identical sheets of paper of dimensions 5 cm by 8 cm.\n    - The first sheet is placed with its bottom-left corner at the bottom-left corner of the table.\n    - Each subsequent sheet is placed 1 cm higher and 1 cm to the right of the previous sheet.\n    - The last sheet touches the top-right corner of the table.\n    - We need to find the length \\( x \\) of the table in cm.\n\n2. **Analyzing the sheets' placement**:\n    - Each new sheet is 1 cm higher and 1 cm to the right of the previous one.\n    - The width of the table is \\( 80 \\) cm.\n    - Initially, the bottom-left corner of the first sheet is at \\( (0, 0) \\).\n    - The right edge of the first sheet is at \\( 8 \\) cm, and the top edge at \\( 5 \\) cm.\n\n3. **Progressing with each step**:\n    - The width starts at 8 cm and increases by 1 cm with each added sheet.\n    - The height starts at 5 cm and increases by 1 cm with each added sheet.\n\n4. **Identifying the total sheets added**:\n    - Final width of the table is \\( 80 \\) cm.\n    - Initial width was \\( 8 \\) cm.\n    - Thus, total increment in width over the whole process:\n      \\[\n      80 - 8 = 72 \\, \\text{cm}\n      \\]\n    - Hence, number of sheets added:\n      \\[\n      72 \\text{ sheets}\n      \\]\n\n5. **Calculating the final height**:\n    - The initial height was \\( 5 \\) cm.\n    - Since the height increases by 1 cm with each sheet addition:\n      \\[\n      \\text{Final height} = 5 + 72 = 77 \\, \\text{cm}\n      \\]\n\n6. **Conclusion**:\n   - The length \\( x \\) of the table is therefore:\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{77 \\, \\text{cm}}\n   \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\nThe length \\( x \\) of the table is \\( 77 \\) cm, hence the final boxed answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{77}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Let there be two circles $\\Gamma_{1}$ and $\\Gamma_{2}$ intersecting at two points $C$ and $D$. Consider a common tangent to these two circles, with its point of tangency at $\\Gamma_{1}$ being $A$ and its point of tangency at $\\Gamma_{2}$ being $B$. Show that the line segment $(CD)$ bisects the line segment $[AB]$ at its midpoint.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to show that the line segment CD bisects the segment AB at its midpoint. Let me start by drawing a diagram to visualize the problem. There are two circles, \u0393\u2081 and \u0393\u2082, intersecting at points C and D. They have a common tangent that touches \u0393\u2081 at A and \u0393\u2082 at B. I need to prove that the line CD passes through the midpoint of AB.\n\nFirst, let me recall some properties of circles and tangents. A tangent to a circle is perpendicular to the radius at the point of tangency. So, if OA is the radius of \u0393\u2081 at A, then OA is perpendicular to the tangent line AB. Similarly, if OB is the radius of \u0393\u2082 at B, then OB is perpendicular to the tangent AB. Wait, but AB is the common tangent, so actually, both OA and OB are radii perpendicular to AB. However, since \u0393\u2081 and \u0393\u2082 are two different circles, their centers are different. Let me denote the centers of \u0393\u2081 and \u0393\u2082 as O\u2081 and O\u2082 respectively. Therefore, O\u2081A is perpendicular to AB, and O\u2082B is also perpendicular to AB. That means O\u2081A and O\u2082B are both perpendicular to the same line AB, so they must be parallel to each other. Hmm, that's an important observation. So O\u2081A is parallel to O\u2082B.\n\nSince O\u2081A and O\u2082B are both perpendicular to AB, they