 + (0 - r\u2081)^2 - r\u2081\u00b2 = m\u00b2 + r\u2081\u00b2 - r\u2081\u00b2 = m\u00b2.\n\nSimilarly, power with respect to \u0393\u2082: |MO\u2082|\u00b2 - r\u2082\u00b2 = (m - 2m)^2 + (0 - r\u2082)^2 - r\u2082\u00b2 = (-m)^2 + r\u2082\u00b2 - r\u2082\u00b2 = m\u00b2.\n\nTherefore, the power of M with respect to both circles is the same (m\u00b2), hence M lies on the radical axis CD. Therefore, CD passes through M, the midpoint of AB. Hence, CD bisects AB at its midpoint.\n\nWait, that seems straightforward. So by using coordinates and computing the power, it shows that M is on CD. Therefore, the conclusion follows. But let me verify if this approach is valid.\n\nYes, because the radical axis is indeed the set of points with equal power, and if M has equal power, then M is on CD. Therefore, CD passes through M, which is the midpoint. So, that's the proof.\n\nBut let me think if there is a way to do this without coordinates, maybe using geometry theorems.\n\nAlternatively, consider that since O\u2081A is perpendicular to AB and O\u2082B is perpendicular to AB, as before, O\u2081A and O\u2082B are both perpendicular to the same line, hence parallel. Therefore, the quadrilateral O\u2081AO\u2082B is a trapezoid with O\u2081A || O\u2082B.\n\nLet M be the midpoint of AB. Let's connect O\u2081 to O\u2082 and consider the line O\u2081O\u2082. The midpoint of O\u2081O\u2082 would be somewhere, but perhaps we can connect M to this.\n\nAlternatively, since O\u2081A and O\u2082B are parallel, the line joining the centers O\u2081O\u2082 is divided proportionally by the midpoint of AB. Wait, maybe using the midline theorem. The line joining the midpoints of the legs of a trapezoid is parallel to the bases and equal to the average of their lengths. Wait, but here AB is not a leg, but the other side.\n\nWait, in the trapezoid O\u2081AO\u2082B, the bases are O\u2081A and O\u2082B, which are vertical (assuming AB is horizontal). The legs are O\u2081O\u2082 and AB. Wait, no, actually