\), leading to the conclusion that \\( f(a) = a \\).\n\n5. **General proof**:\n   - Using the conditions that \\( f(a) \\) divides \\( a^2 \\) and \\( f(a) + 1 \\) divides \\( (a - 1)(a + 1) \\), we show that the only solution is \\( f(a) = a \\).\n\nAfter thorough analysis and verification for various values and conditions, we conclude that the only function satisfying the given condition is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{f(a) = a}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\nTo determine all functions \\( f: \\mathbb{Z}^{+} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{Z}^{+} \\) such that \\( a^2 + f(a) f(b) \\) is divisible by \\( f(a) + b \\) for all positive integers \\( a \\) and \\( b \\), we will proceed as follows:\n\n1. **Substitution $a = b = 1$:**\n    \\[\n    a^2 + f(a) f(b) = 1^2 + f(1) f(1) = 1 + f(1)^2\n    \\]\n    We need:\n    \\[\n    f(1) + 1 \\mid f(1)^2 + 1\n    \\]\n    Let's denote \\( f(1) = c \\). This translates to:\n    \\[\n    c + 1 \\mid c^2 + 1\n    \\]\n    By the divisibility rule, \\( c + 1 \\mid c^2 + 1 \\), implies:\n    \\[\n    c + 1 \\mid (c^2 + 1) - (c + 1)c \\Rightarrow c + 1 \\mid 1 - c \\Rightarrow c + 1 \\leq |1 - c|\n    \\]\n    The only integer solution to this within the positive integers is:\n    \\[\n    c = 1 \\Rightarrow f(1) = 1\n    \\]\n\n2. **Substitution $a = 1$:**\n    \\[\n    1^2 + f(1) f(b) = 1 + f(b)\n    \\]\n    We need:\n    \\[\n    b + 1 \\mid 1 + f(b)\n    \\]\n    Since \\(f(1) = 1\\), we have:\n    \\[\n    b + 1 \\mid f(b) + 1\n    \\]\n    This implies:\n    \\[\n    f(b) \\geq b\n    \\]\n\n3. **Substitution $b = 1$:**\n    \\[\n    a^2 + f(a) f(1) = a^2 + f(a)\n    \\]\n    We need:\n    \\[\n    f(a) + 1 \\mid a^2 + f(a)\n    \\]\n    This can be rewritten as:\n    \\[\n    f(a) + 1 \\mid a^2 - 1\n    \\]\n    Therefore,\n    \\[\n    f(a) \\leq a^2 - 2 \\quad \\text{for all } a \\geq 2\n    \\]\n\n4. **Considering odd primes $p$:**\n    Let \\( p \\) be any odd prime. Substituting \\( a = p \\) and \\( b = f(p) \\) into the original equation:\n    \\[\n    p^2 + f(p) f(f(p))\n    \\]\n    We need:\n    \\[\n    f(p) + f(p) \\mid p^2 + f(p) f(f(p))\n    \\]\n    This implies:\n    \\[\n    2 f(p) \\mid p^2 + f(p) f(f(p))\n    \\]\n    Thus,\n    \\[\n    f(p) \\mid p^2\n    \\]\n    The possible values of \\( f(p) \\) are \\( 1, p, p^2 \\). But from previous steps:\n    \\[\n    p \\leq f(p) \\leq p^2 - 2\n    \\]\n    Hence,\n    \\[\n    f(p) = p\n    \\]\n\n5. **Generalize for all positive integers $b$:**\n    Let's fix \\( b \\) and suppose \\( b \\) can be any positive integer. Substituting \\( a = p \\), we have:\n    \\[\n    b + p \\mid p^2 + p f(b)\n    \\]\n    Rewriting:\n    \\[\n    (b + p) \\mid p^2 + p f(b) - p (b + p) = p^2 + p f(b) - p b - p^2 = p(f(b) - b)\n    \\]\n    Since \\( b + p \\) is arbitrary large, we have:\n    \\[\n    b f(b) - b^2 = 0 \\Rightarrow f(b) = b\n    \\]\n\nThus, the only function satisfying all these conditions is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{f(n) = n \\text{ for all } n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^{+}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Prove that the line of intersection of two planes, which are perpendicular to a third plane, is perpendicular to the third plane.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Alright, so I need to prove that if two planes are each perpendicular to a third plane, then their line of intersection is also perpendicular to that third plane. Hmm, let me start by recalling some basic concepts about planes in 3D geometry. \n\nFirst, I know that two planes can either be parallel or intersect along a line. Since the problem states that there are two planes perpendicular to a third one, they must intersect because if they were parallel and both perpendicular to the third plane, they would be parallel to each other, but the problem doesn't mention that. So, they intersect along a line. The key is to show that this line of intersection is itself perpendicular to the third plane.\n\nLet me think about the 