h is the integral from 0 to T of [g(x) - f(x)] dx, where T is the period of f(x).\n\nSo, first, I need to determine the period of f(x). The function f(x) is a combination of sin(ax) and cos(ax), so the period of both sin(ax) and cos(ax) is 2\u03c0 / a. Therefore, the period of f(x) is also 2\u03c0 / a. That makes sense because when you combine sine and cosine functions with the same argument, the period remains the same as the individual functions.\n\nTherefore, the smallest positive period is T = 2\u03c0 / a.\n\nNow, the next step is to compute the area between f(x) and g(x) over this interval [0, 2\u03c0/a]. Since g(x) is a constant function equal to the amplitude of f(x), the area will be the integral from 0 to T of [sqrt(a\u00b2 + 1) - (a sin(ax) + cos(ax))] dx.\n\nTo compute this integral, let me write it out:\n\nArea = \u222b\u2080^{2\u03c0/a} [sqrt(a\u00b2 + 1) - a sin(ax) - cos(ax)] dx\n\nThis integral should be straightforward to compute term by term.\n\nFirst, integrate sqrt(a\u00b2 + 1) over 0 to 2\u03c0/a. That's simply sqrt(a\u00b2 + 1) * (2\u03c0/a).\n\nSecond, integrate -a sin(ax) over 0 to 2\u03c0/a. The integral of sin(ax) dx is -cos(ax)/a, so multiplying by -a gives:\n\n-a * [-cos(ax)/a] evaluated from 0 to 2\u03c0/a = cos(ax) evaluated from 0 to 2\u03c0/a.\n\nAt 2\u03c0/a: cos(a * 2\u03c0/a) = cos(2\u03c0) = 1.\n\nAt 0: cos(0) = 1.\n\nSo the integral becomes 1 - 1 = 0. Therefore, the second term contributes 0.\n\nThird, integrate -cos(ax) over 0 to 2\u03c0/a. The integral of cos(ax) dx is sin(ax)/a, so the integral becomes:\n\n- [sin(ax)/a] evaluated from 0 to 2\u03c0/a = - [sin(2\u03c0)/a - sin(0)/a] = - [0 - 0] = 0.\n\nTherefore, both the second and third terms integrate to 0. Therefore, the entire area is just sqrt(a\u00b2 + 1) * (2\u03c0/a).\n\nBut wait, that can't be right. Because if I just integrate the constant sqrt(a\u00b2 + 1) over the period, and subtract the integral of f(x) which integrates to 0, then the area is sqrt(a\u00b2 +1) * (2\u03c0/a). But is that the case?\n\nWait, let me check again. The integral of f(x) over one period:\n\n\u222b\u2080^{2\u03c0/a} [a sin(ax) + cos(ax)] dx\n\nCompute integral term by term:\n\nIntegral of a sin(ax) dx = -cos(ax) evaluated from 0 to 2\u03c0/a: -cos(2\u03c0) + cos(0) = -1 + 1 = 0.\n\nIntegral of cos(ax) dx = (1/a) sin(ax) evaluated from 0 to 2\u03c0/a: (1/a)(sin(2\u03c0) - sin(0)) = 0.\n\nSo yes, the integral of f(x) over one period is 0. Therefore, the area is sqrt(a\u00b2 +1)*(2\u03c0/a) - 0 = (2\u03c0/a)*sqrt(a\u00b2 +1).\n\nBut wait, that seems too straightforward. However, this is because f(x) is a sinusoidal function with average value 0 over its period, so the integral of f(x) over a full period is 0. Therefore, the area between f(x) and g(x) is just the integral of the constant g(x) over the period minus 0, which is (sqrt(a\u00b2 +1))*(2\u03c0/a).\n\nBut hold on, the problem says \"the closed region formed by the graph of f(x) over its smallest positive period and the graph of g(x)\". But if f(x) reaches up to g(x) at its maximum, then the region between them is actually the area where f(x) is below g(x). But since f(x) oscillates and touches g(x) at certain points, the area between them should be the integral of [g(x) - f(x)] over the interval where f(x) \u2264 g(x). However, since f(x) has a period T, and it's symmetric, the region between 0 and T would be enclosed by f(x) and g(x) only if f(x) is below g(x) throughout the period. Wait, but actually, the maximum of f(x) is equal to g(x), so the graphs touch at the maxima, but elsewhere f(x) is less than g(x). Therefore, over the entire period, f(x) \u2264 g(x), and they touch at the maxima. Therefore, the closed region is the area between f(x) and g(x) over one period, which is indeed the integral from 0 to T of [g(x) - f(x)] dx.\n\nBut according to the calculations above, that integral is (2\u03c0/a)*sqrt(a\u00b2 +1). But let me check with an example. Suppose a = 1. Then f(x) = sin(x) + cos(x)