rom 0 to1. Therefore,\n\n\u222b\u2080^{f(1)} y / f\u2019(f\u207b\u00b9(y)) dy = \u222b\u2080\u00b9 f(z) dz =I,\n\nwhich we already knew. Similarly,\n\n\u222b\u2080^{f(1)} y^3 / f\u2019(f\u207b\u00b9(y)) dy = \u222b\u2080\u00b9 f(z)^3 dz.\n\nSo, this substitution shows that the integrals are the same as the original ones, so this approach doesn't yield new information.\n\nMaybe going back to the original problem, with the given conditions, and trying to apply an inequality.\n\nLet\u2019s consider the function f and compare it to x. Since f(x) \u2264x, and f\u2019(x) \u22641. Maybe consider writing f(x) =x - h(x), where h(x) \u22650. Then, f\u2019(x) =1 - h\u2019(x). Since f\u2019(x) \u2208(0,1], then h\u2019(x) \u2208 [0,1). Also, h(0)=0 because f(0)=0. So, h(x) =\u222b\u2080^x h\u2019(t) dt \u22650. But not sure if this helps.\n\nExpress the integrals in terms of h(x):\n\n\u222b\u2080\u00b9 f(x) dx = \u222b\u2080\u00b9 (x - h(x)) dx =1/2 - \u222b\u2080\u00b9 h(x) dx.\n\n\u222b\u2080\u00b9 f(x)^3 dx = \u222b\u2080\u00b9 (x - h(x))^3 dx = \u222b\u2080\u00b9 x\u00b3 -3x\u00b2 h(x) +3x h(x)^2 -h(x)^3 dx.\n\n=1/4 -3 \u222bx\u00b2 h dx +3 \u222bx h\u00b2 dx - \u222bh\u00b3 dx.\n\nThe inequality to show is:\n\n(1/2 - \u222bh dx)^2 \u22651/4 -3 \u222bx\u00b2 h dx +3 \u222bx h\u00b2 dx - \u222bh\u00b3 dx.\n\nExpanding the left side:\n\n(1/2)^2 - (1/2)(2 \u222bh dx) + (\u222bh dx)^2 =1/4 - \u222bh dx + (\u222bh dx)^2.\n\nSo, the inequality becomes:\n\n1/4 - \u222bh dx + (\u222bh dx)^2 \u22651/4 -3 \u222bx\u00b2 h dx +3 \u222bx h\u00b2 dx - \u222bh\u00b3 dx.\n\nSubtract 1/4 from both sides:\n\n-\u222bh dx + (\u222bh dx)^2 \u2265 -3 \u222bx\u00b2 h dx +3 \u222bx h\u00b2 dx - \u222bh\u00b3 dx.\n\nBring all terms to the left:\n\n-\u222bh dx + (\u222bh dx)^2 +3 \u222bx\u00b2 h dx -3 \u222bx h\u00b2 dx + \u222bh\u00b3 dx \u22650.\n\nThis seems very complicated. Maybe not the right path.\n\nAlternative idea: Since f\u2019(x) \u22641 and f\u2019(x) >0, then the inverse function f\u20