n   \\[\n   6 f(0) - 9 f^2(0) \\geq 1\n   \\]\n   Similarly, solving the quadratic inequality as above:\n   \\[\n   f(0) = \\frac{1}{3}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Substituting \\( y = z = 1 \\):**\n   \\[\n   3 f(x \\cdot 1) + 3 f(x \\cdot 1) - 9 f(x) f(1 \\cdot 1) \\geq 1\n   \\]\n   Simplifies to:\n   \\[\n   6 f(x) - 9 f(x) \\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\geq 1 \\implies 6 f(x) - 3 f(x) \\geq 1 \\implies 3 f(x) \\geq 1\n   \\]\n   Hence:\n   \\[\n   f(x) \\geq \\frac{1}{3}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Substituting \\( y = z = 0 \\):**\n   \\[\n   3 f(x \\cdot 0) + 3 f(x \\cdot 0) - 9 f(x) f(0 \\cdot 0) \\geq 1\n   \\]\n   Simplifies to:\n   \\[\n   6 f(0) - 9 f(x) \\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\geq 1 \\implies 2 - 3 f(x) \\geq 1\n   \\]\n   Hence:\n   \\[\n   2 \\geq 3 f(x) \\implies f(x) \\leq \\frac{1}{3}\n   \\]\n\nFrom steps 4 and 5, we have:\n\\[\nf(x) = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\n6. **Finding the sum \\( \\sum_{i=1}^{100} [i f(i)] \\):**\n\nSince \\( f(i) = \\frac{1}{3} \\):\n\\[\ni f(i) = \\frac{i}{3}\n\\]\n\nSo, \\( [i f(i)] = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{i}{3} \\right\\rfloor \\).\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{100} \\left\\lfloor \\frac{i}{3} \\right\\rfloor\n\\]\n\nEvaluating \\( \\left\\lfloor \\frac{i}{3} \\right\\rfloor \\) for \\( i = 1 \\) to \\( 100 \\):\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{100} \\left\\lfloor \\frac{i}{3} \\right\\rfloor = \\sum_{i=1}^{100} \\left\\lfloor \\frac{i}{3} \\right\\rfloor\n\\]\n\nBreaking it down into groups:\n- \\( i = 1, 2, 3 \\) gives 0, 0, 1 respectively.\n- \\( i = 4, 5, 6 \\) gives 1, 1, 2 respectively.\n- Continue this pattern up to \\( 100 \\).\n\nRecognizing that every 3 consecutive terms contribute a partial sum:\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{1}{3} \\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{2}{3} \\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{3}{3} \\right\\rfloor = 0 + 0 + 1 = 1\n\\]\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{4}{3} \\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{5}{3} \\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{6}{3} \\right\\rfloor = 1 + 1 + 2 = 4\n\\]\nEach three terms gives approximately 3/2 on average. Rec